# Line endings are decided here, not by whatever core.autocrlf each machine
# happens to have set.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true commits CRLF
# into any file git has already stored with CRLF -- autocrlf skips normalising
# those, so the first one to slip through is permanent and spreads. 103 files
# had drifted that way, and it broke CI in two places at once:
#
#   * `gofmt -l .` reports every CRLF Go file, failing the formatting gate on
#     four of the six module jobs. Since that step runs before build, vet and
#     test, none of those ever ran.
#   * `prettier --check` does the same for 58 frontend files, failing the
#     Frontend job at its second step.
#
# Neither was a code problem -- the same files pass both tools the moment the
# line endings are LF. But the failure reads as "the build is broken", and the
# integration job (needs: [build, migrations]) never starts, so one setting
# presents as the whole pipeline being red.
#
# `eol=lf` normalises the working copy too, not just the stored blob. That is
# deliberate: the Go services, the frontend and the Docker images are all built
# on Linux, so LF is what everything downstream expects, and matching it here
# means the diff a Windows machine produces is the diff CI sees.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts are the exception -- they are Windows-only by definition
# and CRLF is the convention there.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

*.ico binary
